      'Admitting a mistake threatens identity, ego, and the story we have already invested in. The psychology of defending bad decisions is not about pride — it is about self-coherence.',
    content: `Everyone has watched someone defend a decision that was clearly wrong. Maybe it was a colleague insisting a project was on track. A friend justifying a relationship everyone could see was damaging. A public figure doubling down on a position that had been thoroughly discredited. The behaviour frustrates onlookers, who see the admission of error as simple and obvious. From inside the person making the decision, it is not simple at all.

When a person makes a decision, they do not just choose an action. They build a narrative around it. They decide, and then they tell themselves why the decision was reasonable. They construct reasons. They anticipate outcomes. They commit to a version of the future that makes the decision look good. This narrative construction happens quickly and largely unconsciously. Within hours, sometimes minutes, the decision has been woven into the person's self-story. It is no longer just a choice. It is part of who they are.

Admitting the decision was wrong means unraveling that part of the story. It means the reasons were bad, the anticipation was flawed, and the version of the future they committed to was wrong. For most people, this does not feel like updating a spreadsheet. It feels like admitting they are not the person they thought they were — someone who makes good decisions, who sees things clearly, who can be trusted to judge well. The threat to identity is real and it is immediate.

The stronger the initial commitment, the harder the admission. If someone made the decision publicly, the cost of reversal is higher because it involves social face. If they invested significant resources — time, money, reputation — the sunk cost intensifies the need to justify. If they persuaded others to follow the decision, admitting it was wrong means admitting they led people astray. Each of these factors adds weight to the defense.

The defense itself usually looks rational from the inside. The person does not think they are being defensive. They think they are being accurate. They point to data that supports their position. They highlight ambiguities in the evidence against them. They reframe the criteria for success so the decision looks better. None of this is deliberate dishonesty. The mind is genuinely adjusting the perception to maintain coherence. The person believes the adjusted version because the alternative — that they made a significant error — is too costly to accept.

Over time, if the evidence against the decision becomes overwhelming, the person might gradually shift their position. But the shift is rarely a clean admission. It is more like a slow retreat, with qualifiers and conditions that allow the person to save face. The decision was right given what I knew at the time. The situation changed. There were factors nobody could have predicted. These statements may be true, and they also serve the function of softening the blow to identity.

The people who can admit mistakes most easily are the ones whose identity is not tightly bound to being right. They have separated their self-worth from their decision-making accuracy. This is not common, and it is not easy. Being right feels good, and most people have spent their lives being rewarded for having correct answers. The association between rightness and worth is deeply embedded. Untangling it takes time and usually requires enough experiences of being wrong and surviving that the fear diminishes. The person learns that admitting an error does not destroy them. It is uncomfortable, but it is survivable. That knowledge, earned through experience, is what makes the admission possible.`
